  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather is expected through the middle\nof the week with climbing temperatures.\n\nWhile some breaks in the clouds have developed, the 12Z\nIAD sounding showed lots of moisture trapped below a subsidence\ninversion. Expecting those breaks in the clouds to fill in \nagain this afternoon. \n\nClouds will hold temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s, \nexcept mid 70s in central Virginia where there may be more \nbreaks of sun. Winds will be lighter than the weekend as the \npressure gradient relaxes.\n\nSome clouds likely linger through tonight. Some fog may develop\nwest of the Blue Ridge, if there are breaks. Lows will largely\nbe in the 50s.\n\nRidging at the surface and aloft will build toward the eastern\nUS for the middle of the week, leading to a steady warming\ntrend. Residual moisture may remain trapped beneath the\nsubsidence inversion with weak gradients on Tuesday, although\ndeeper mixing will likely support more breaks of sun. While dry\nconditions are most likely, it's worth noting some guidance\nindicates there may be a brief shower or sprinkle as the\nmid/upper level trough axis will still be overhead. Highs will\nbe closer to normal in the 70s.\n\nTemperatures will climb further Wednesday and Thursday, with\nmore locations reaching the 80s. Some spots could even reach the\nupper 80s by Thursday. Developing southerly flow will lead to\nmore sunshine. Dry weather is expected both days. With light \nwinds and elevated dew points, some patchy fog could form during\nthe overnight hours.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will approach on Friday, leading to\ncooler and potentially unsettled weather for the weekend.\n\nExpect continued warm conditions Friday in response to the \nabove average heights across the region. Unlike many ridges that\nstagnate during the summertime, this feature is forecast to be \nmore transient in nature while edging off the Atlantic coast by \nFriday. Amplifying flow in the northern stream will allow a \nlongwave trough to graze the area by Friday into Saturday. Given\nthe height falls are rather weak, the accompanying cold front\nwhich reaches the area on Friday likely stalls just off to the \nsouth through the weekend. The pattern turns a bit more \nunpredictable by Sunday into early next week. An elongated area \nof vorticity spinning initially around the Southern Plains may \nbegin to edge northeastward in time. Enhanced vertical motions \nplus increasing moisture fluxes would support a risk for more \nconcentrated rainfall. However, uncertainty is rather high given\nthe mesoscale nature of these features.\n\nAs the weak surface boundary nears the area, rain chances \nreturn to the forecast for Friday. Given the weak nature of the \nfront and limited upper- level support, such chances are largely\ncapped around 20 to 40 percent. As mentioned above, this \nfrontal system is likely to linger just off to the south for the\nweekend. This will maintain a continued threat for rain along \nwith cooler temperatures. At this point, daily highs are \nforecast to be in the 60s, with 50s at night given the abundant \nclouds and further rain chances.",
